From: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>
Subject: [PATCH] _Fork: fail on FDPIC targets without SYS_fork

An architecture with the generic syscall table has no fork, and _Fork
falls back to clone(SIGCHLD, 0).  A no-MMU kernel accepts that, but the
child gets no copy of the parent's memory: it runs on the parent's stack
and data, and both corrupt each other.  On FDPIC, which is musl's no-MMU
model, report ENOSYS, as SH FDPIC's kernel does for its fork.

Signed-off-by: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>

diff --git a/src/process/_Fork.c b/src/process/_Fork.c
index 9c07792..5ff6d9b 100644
--- a/src/process/_Fork.c
+++ b/src/process/_Fork.c
@@ -1,5 +1,6 @@
 #include <unistd.h>
 #include <signal.h>
+#include <errno.h>
 #include "syscall.h"
 #include "libc.h"
 #include "lock.h"
@@ -34,6 +35,10 @@ pid_t _Fork(void)
 	LOCK(__abort_lock);
 #ifdef SYS_fork
 	ret = __syscall(SYS_fork);
+#elif defined(__FDPIC__)
+	/* Without an MMU, a clone without CLONE_VM gets no copy of the
+	 * parent's memory: it would run on the parent's stack and data. */
+	ret = -ENOSYS;
 #else
 	ret = __syscall(SYS_clone, SIGCHLD, 0);
 #endif
